WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.389
So far, all our data has lived in variables.

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:06.900
And when the program ends, the variables are gone.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:14.970
A file keeps data on the disk, after the program has finished, and for the next program to read.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.321
Here's part of a hosts file.

00:00:17.321 --> 00:00:22.010
It's a text file, and a text file can be thought of as a sequence of lines.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.320
This is Lecture 4, part 4: reading and writing files.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:31.751
We'll open a file, see what really separates its lines,

00:00:31.751 --> 00:00:35.312
read it line by line and all at once, search it,

00:00:35.312 --> 00:00:37.390
and write a file of our own.

00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:41.153
To have a file to read, our first program writes one.

00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:43.744
We'll explain writing at the end of this part.

00:00:43.744 --> 00:00:44.927
For now, just run it.

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:50.491
It writes the 6 lines of the hosts file into a new file called myhost.txt,

00:00:50.491 --> 00:00:52.957
in the folder where you run Python.

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:59.404
Before reading a file, we must tell Python which file, and what we'll do with it.

00:00:59.404 --> 00:01:02.134
The built-in function `open` does this.

00:01:02.484 --> 00:01:07.001
`open` takes a file name, which is a string, and a mode.

00:01:07.001 --> 00:01:12.084
The mode is 'r' to read, which is the default, or 'w' to write.

00:01:12.434 --> 00:01:14.886
`open` returns a file handle.

00:01:14.886 --> 00:01:23.682
Not the file's contents, but an object we use to work with the file: to read it, write it, and close it.

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:27.234
It's like File > Open in a word processor.

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:31.967
In fact, if you print the handle, you don't see any of the text.

00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:35.714
You see what kind of object it is, its name, and its mode.

00:01:36.064 --> 00:01:41.464
And if the file doesn't exist, `open` raises a `FileNotFoundError`.

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:45.305
Now, what separates the lines of a file?

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:48.300
A special character called the newline.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.834
In a string, we write it as `\n`.

00:01:51.184 --> 00:01:55.024
Type the string "Hello,\nWorld" at the prompt,

00:01:55.024 --> 00:01:59.784
and Python shows it back with the `\n` inside the quotes.

00:02:00.134 --> 00:02:03.826
But when we print it, the newline starts a new line.

00:02:03.826 --> 00:02:06.454
Hello on one line, World on the next.

00:02:06.804 --> 00:02:11.517
And `\n` is one character, not two.

00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:14.884
`X\nY` has a length of 3.

00:02:15.484 --> 00:02:21.604
So a text file is really one long string, with a newline at the end of each line.

00:02:22.401 --> 00:02:28.202
A file handle opened for reading behaves like a sequence of strings, one per line.

00:02:28.202 --> 00:02:31.881
So a `for` loop goes through the file, line by line.

00:02:32.231 --> 00:02:36.831
This loop opens `myhost.txt`, and prints each line.

00:02:37.181 --> 00:02:42.624
Remember: each line of the file ends with a newline, and `print` adds its own.

00:02:42.624 --> 00:02:44.577
Pause the video and predict.

00:02:44.577 --> 00:02:46.741
What does the output look like?

00:02:47.291 --> 00:02:50.211
Every line is followed by a blank line.

00:02:51.001 --> 00:02:55.073
Each line arrives with its newline still on the end.

00:02:55.073 --> 00:02:58.283
`print` shows it, and then adds one more.

00:02:58.283 --> 00:03:02.121
Two newlines, so there's a blank line in between.

00:03:02.721 --> 00:03:06.373
The fix is the string method from part 3: `rstrip`.

00:03:06.373 --> 00:03:11.601
It removes whitespace, newlines included, from the right end of the line.

00:03:11.951 --> 00:03:16.631
Strip each line, then print it, and the output looks just like the file.

00:03:16.981 --> 00:03:20.337
Remember this when you compare lines as well.

00:03:20.337 --> 00:03:25.261
A line still ending in `\n` is never equal to the text without it.

00:03:26.034 --> 00:03:29.373
The slides' practice problem is to count the lines of a file.

00:03:29.373 --> 00:03:31.234
That's the counting pattern again.

00:03:31.584 --> 00:03:34.193
Set `count` to 0 before the loop.

00:03:34.193 --> 00:03:35.775
Add 1 for each line.

00:03:35.775 --> 00:03:38.384
Print the `count` after the loop.

00:03:38.734 --> 00:03:40.854
Our file has 6 lines.

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:44.316
You can also read the whole file at once.

00:03:44.316 --> 00:03:49.354
The `read` method gives the whole file as a single string, newlines included.

00:03:49.704 --> 00:03:53.504
Our file is 155 characters long.

00:03:53.854 --> 00:03:57.492
And its first 20 characters print over two lines.

00:03:57.492 --> 00:03:57.789
Why?

00:03:57.789 --> 00:04:05.214
They are the 17 characters of the first line, then the newline, then 21, the start of the next line.

00:04:05.564 --> 00:04:08.905
Reading everything at once is handy for small files.

00:04:08.905 --> 00:04:14.044
For large ones, loop line by line, so the whole file never has to sit in memory.

00:04:14.834 --> 00:04:20.194
To search a file, put an `if` inside the loop, and keep only the lines you want.

00:04:20.544 --> 00:04:25.708
This loop strips each line, and prints it only if it starts with a hash sign.

00:04:25.708 --> 00:04:30.000
Those are the comment lines: Gmail web start, and Gmail web end.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.744
Then it prints 'finished'.

00:04:32.094 --> 00:04:36.161
The slide writes `if line.startswith('#') == True`.

00:04:36.161 --> 00:04:38.474
You don't need the `== True`.

00:04:38.474 --> 00:04:44.774
`startswith` already gives `True` or `False`, which is exactly what `if` needs.

00:04:45.124 --> 00:04:52.610
This is the pattern most file programs in this course start with: read, strip, filter.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:58.964
Loop over the lines, strip each one, test it, and use the ones that pass.

00:04:59.734 --> 00:05:01.020
Finally, writing.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:06.014
Open the file with mode 'w', and call the handle's `write` method.

00:05:06.364 --> 00:05:10.973
The `write` method does not add a newline for you.

00:05:10.973 --> 00:05:14.844
Put `\n` at the end of each line yourself.

00:05:15.444 --> 00:05:19.204
Then close the file when you're done, so everything is saved.

00:05:19.554 --> 00:05:21.266
Careful with mode 'w'.

00:05:21.266 --> 00:05:26.714
It creates the file, or, if the file already exists, empties it first.

00:05:27.064 --> 00:05:30.784
Read it back to check, and there are our 3 lines.

00:05:31.134 --> 00:05:34.675
You'll also see a `with` statement in many programs.

00:05:34.675 --> 00:05:39.237
It closes the file for you, even if an error happens in the middle.

00:05:39.237 --> 00:05:43.254
In this course we use `open` and `close`, as the slides do.

00:05:44.034 --> 00:05:48.492
An AI assistant can write code that reads and writes files in seconds.

00:05:48.492 --> 00:05:51.994
Check what it gives you, with the tools from this part.

00:05:52.344 --> 00:05:53.579
Newlines first.

00:05:53.579 --> 00:05:57.941
Does the code `rstrip` each line before comparing it?

00:05:57.941 --> 00:06:00.904
And does it add `\n` when it writes?

00:06:01.254 --> 00:06:06.003
Then try the edge cases: an empty file, and a file that doesn't exist.

00:06:06.003 --> 00:06:08.174
What does the code do with each?

00:06:08.524 --> 00:06:15.004
And if it writes with mode 'w', make sure it isn't about to empty a file you wanted to keep.

00:06:15.801 --> 00:06:17.281
Let's sum up.

00:06:17.631 --> 00:06:19.701
`open` gives you a file handle.

00:06:19.701 --> 00:06:22.973
A `for` loop over it reads the file line by line,

00:06:22.973 --> 00:06:25.511
and `read` gives it all as one string.

00:06:25.861 --> 00:06:29.399
Every line that comes from a file still ends with a newline.

00:06:29.399 --> 00:06:31.581
Strip it before you print or compare.

00:06:31.931 --> 00:06:35.090
Mode 'w' creates or empties a file,

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:38.611
and `write` adds no newline of its own.

00:06:38.961 --> 00:06:40.320
Here's one to try.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.757
Write a function, `count_lines_with`, that takes a file name and a word,

00:06:45.757 --> 00:06:51.194
and returns how many lines of the file contain that word, ignoring case.

00:06:51.194 --> 00:06:53.761
Test it on a file you write first.

00:06:54.311 --> 00:06:56.381
That's the end of Lecture 4.

00:06:56.381 --> 00:07:02.591
In Lecture 5, we meet lists, dictionaries and tuples: many values, kept in one name.

